{{also|Buddy}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=buddy}}

===Etymology===
1802, colloquial {{m|en|butty||[[companion]]}}, also the form of an older dialect term meaning [[workmate]], associated with coal mining. Itself believed derived from 1530 as ''booty fellow'', a partner with whom one shares [[booty]] or [[loot]].<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|accessdate=November 2008}}</ref>

Sometimes referred to in North American dictionaries as an alteration of {{m|en|brother}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bʌ.di/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-buddy.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌdi|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|buddies}}

# A [[friend]] or [[casual]] acquaintance.
#: ''They have been '''buddies''' since they were in school.''
# A [[partner]] for a particular activity.
#: ''drinking '''buddies'''''
# An [[informal]] and friendly address to a [[stranger]]; a friendly [[placeholder]] name for a person one does not know.
#: ''Hey, '''buddy''', I think you dropped this.''

